<h2 id="connecting">Connecting</h2>
<div class="table">
    <table>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Endpoint</strong></td><td><code>http://&lt;server&gt;:9000</code> on your network, or your HTTPS name from outside (<a href="docs/dbnsync#internet">how</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bucket</strong></td><td><code>dbn</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td><code>us-east-1</code>; RustFS ignores it, but clients want one</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Addressing</strong></td><td>Path-style</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Key</strong></td><td>A read-only key for the bucket (<a href="docs/dbnsync#keys">how to make one</a>)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="layout">Layout</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="The bucket" Text="@Layout" />
<ul>
    <li>Keys follow <code>GLBX.MDP3/{schema}/{root}/{yyyy}/{root}.{schema}.{yyyyMMdd}.dbn.zst</code>, one per UTC day.</li>
    <li><code>.empty</code> objects mark days that were asked for and had no data. Skip them when downloading.</li>
    <li><code>_dbnsync/</code> holds the worker's own records of jobs and passes. Readers can ignore it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="files">What is in a file</h2>
<p>
    Each <code>.dbn.zst</code> is one UTC day of one schema for one root, exactly as Databento delivered it:
    zstd-compressed <a href="@Links.DbnFormat">DBN</a>, version 3.
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>The metadata block</strong> says what was requested (<code>MNQ.n.0</code> and <code>MNQ.n.1</code> for <code>mbo</code>; <code>MNQ.FUT</code> for the rest) and maps each instrument id in the file back to it. Records name instruments only by id.</li>
    <li><strong><code>mbo</code></strong> holds only the two leading outrights. Which contract <code>MNQ.n.0</code> is changes at each roll; the mapping in each day's file says which it was that day, and that day's <code>definition</code> file names it.</li>
    <li><strong>Prices</strong> are integers in units of 1e-9, and <strong>times</strong> nanoseconds since the UNIX epoch, UTC. DBN readers convert both.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="aws">AWS CLI</h2>
<CodeBlock Title="Once: use path-style addressing" Text="aws configure set default.s3.addressing_style path" />
<CodeBlock Title="List a month, and sync a series to a folder" Text="@Aws" />

<h2 id="python">Python</h2>
<p>With <code>boto3</code> and Databento's own <code>databento</code> package, which reads DBN into a pandas DataFrame:</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Python" Text="@Python" />

<h2 id="dbn">Databento's dbn tool</h2>
<p>The <code>dbn</code> command (<code>cargo install dbn-cli</code>) turns a downloaded file into CSV or JSON:</p>
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="dbn MNQ.mbo.20261028.dbn.zst --csv --output MNQ.mbo.20261028.csv" />

<h2 id="rclone">rclone</h2>
<p>rclone mounts or syncs the bucket. Configure an S3 remote with provider <code>Other</code>:</p>
<CodeBlock Title="~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf" Text="@Rclone" />
<CodeBlock Title="Any terminal" Text="@RcloneSync" />

